---
/**
 * The Services index — `/servicii` · `/en/services`.
 *
 * OWNERSHIP: Workstream A.
 *
 * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS — AND WHY IT IS NOT AN INVENTION
 * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * It was built to close a 404 on a locked global-nav item, so the first duty of
 * this header is to show that the page was *already specified* and simply never
 * sequenced. Four independent authorities require it:
 *
 *   1. **IA §2.1 sitemap** — "`/servicii`  Services index — opens with the
 *      two-pillar fork", with `/servicii/[serviciu]` nested beneath it.
 *   2. **IA §2.5 page-type responsibilities** — "**Services Index** — orient &
 *      route (forked by pillar); **task-oriented entry point** — forks directly
 *      to grouped Service pages (the Pillar Hub is not a required stop).
 *      *Not:* detail/sell; never a flat list."
 *   3. **NAV_DECISION_RECORD.md §5(1)** — task-first global navigation is valid
 *      "**only while all three hold**", the first being "Servicii and Proiecte
 *      both expose the two pillars internally — each opens with a co-equal
 *      pillar fork, never a flat mixed list." The page is therefore a
 *      *precondition of the locked navigation model*, not an optional extra.
 *   4. **COMPONENT_INVENTORY.md** — Service Preview Card, "*Appears on:* Hub
 *      services overview (H-3), **Services index**." The component this page
 *      composes was assigned to it before either was built.
 *
 * IA Step 6 (M1) adds the intent this page serves: "the **Services index** lets
 * visitors who already know what they need browse grouped Service pages
 * directly, without first passing through the Pillar Hub."
 *
 * ── NO PAGE IA, NO WIREFRAME, NO HiFi — AND WHAT THAT CONSTRAINS ──────────
 * `PAGE_IA_INDEX.md` §2 covers **six** page types and the Services index is not
 * among them; no `docs/pages/services-index/` exists. That is a real gap, and it
 * is reported rather than filled here. Its consequence is a discipline, not a
 * blocker: this page renders **only** what IA §2.5 and NAV §5(1) name — a fork
 * by pillar, and grouped Service links — and nothing a Page IA would otherwise
 * have decided. No hero, no statistics, no comparison, no editorial narrative,
 * no per-service detail. IA §2.5's "*Not:* detail/sell" is the test each element
 * had to pass.
 *
 * **Rhythm.** `tokens.css` has no `services-index` set, and its own note is that
 * "a page type with no approved HiFi … has no rhythm set here and cannot be
 * built until one is authored upstream." None is added — inventing pacing values
 * is the redesign that note prevents. The page composes with `rhythm="pillar-hub"`,
 * an existing authored set, because the hub is the page type this reads most
 * like: an overview that groups Service Preview Cards under section markers.
 * Contact set the same precedent for the same reason. Swapping to a dedicated
 * set when one is authored is one prop.
 *
 * ── THE TAXONOMY IS THE AUTHORED ONE ──────────────────────────────────────
 * Groups come from `PILLARS` (`types.ts`) and each Service's **authored**
 * `pillar` field (IA §2.3, "Service → Pillar"). Pillar display names come from
 * the single declaration in `vocabulary.ts`. Nothing here enumerates services,
 * invents a group, or hardcodes a name — `CONTENT_MODEL.md` §2 makes Service a
 * first-class object the owner authors without touching code, and §6 makes a new
 * service "a new *value* or a new *Service object*, not a new hard-coded section".
 *
 * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  PILLAR MEDIA — SOURCED FROM AN EXISTING CONTRACT, OR NOT AT ALL
 * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * The composition gives each pillar a visual area. No image is invented,
 * downloaded, hardcoded or substituted for it, and no CMS field was added.
 *
 * **The precedent.** The site already has a co-equal two-pillar fork with
 * per-pillar media: the Homepage's M-2. `Homepage.astro` sources it as "M-2's
 * representative media: the leading curated work of each pillar" —
 * `highlights(slot, pillar, locale)[0]`, then `Plate` renders `.cover`. This
 * page does exactly that, one call and one fallback, with no new concept
 * between them.
 *
 * **The slot.** `HIGHLIGHT_SLOTS` is a frozen two-value set — `homepage` and
 * `pillar-hub` — and there is no `services-index` slot. Adding one would be a
 * Workstream B schema change made to satisfy a layout, which is precisely what
 * is forbidden. So the routes read `pillar-hub`: of the two, it is the slot
 * whose meaning is "the work the owner curated to represent this pillar", which
 * is what a pillar-level fork needs. That is a *reuse* of a slot authored for a
 * neighbouring surface, and it is flagged in the report for B and the owner to
 * ratify. If they decline, the plates fall back to their authored tone and no
 * code changes.
 *
 * **What actually renders today.** The dataset currently contains **zero image
 * assets** — no Service `hero`, no Work Entry `cover`, nothing. Three states
 * therefore exist, in this precedence, and the box is the same size in all three:
 *
 *   1. a curated Sanity cover — production, and always wins;
 *   2. under `DEV_VISUAL_MEDIA=true`, a temporary local visual-QA image from this
 *      pillar's own pool (`lib/dev/visual-media.ts`). Never content, never
 *      deployed — `verify-no-dev-media` fails the build that tries;
 *   3. the authored plate with the measurement graticule — the site's existing
 *      treatment for media that does not exist yet, not a placeholder pretending
 *      to be a photograph.
 *
 * The composition is designed for state 1 and degrades to state 3 without moving
 * a pixel of layout, which is what makes it safe to review under state 2. The
 * missing media contract is reported separately rather than papered over.
 *
 * ── EMPTINESS, HONESTLY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A pillar with no published Service in this locale renders **no group** — the
 * precedent the hub's H-3 module set ("an empty Services overview would advertise
 * an offering that has not been published; its absence advertises nothing"), and
 * the reason COMPONENT_INVENTORY.md scopes the Empty State component to the Work
 * Archive and the Service page and to nowhere else. With no services at all, the
 * page renders its orientation and stops: no group scaffolding, no counter, no
 * fabricated "coming soon" claim. The route still resolves, which is what the
 * global nav requires of it.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Plate from '../media/Plate.astro';
import ServicePreviewCard from '../ServicePreviewCard.astro';
import ViewTransition from '../ViewTransition.astro';

import { localize, PILLARS } from '../../lib/content';
import type { Locale, Pillar, ServiceSummary, WorkEntrySummary } from '../../lib/content';
/* DEVELOPMENT ONLY — the pool a pillar's box draws from. Returns a folder name and
   nothing else; `Plate` consults the overlay only after real media has already failed,
   and the overlay is inert without `DEV_VISUAL_MEDIA=true`. */
import { devServicePool, devVisualImage } from '../../lib/dev/visual-media';
import { routePath } from '../../lib/i18n/routes';
import { servicesIndexMessages } from '../../lib/i18n/services-index';
import { pillarLabel, pillarRouteKey } from '../../lib/i18n/vocabulary';

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  /** Locale-scoped and curation-ordered by `ContentSource` before it arrives. */
  services: readonly ServiceSummary[];
  /**
   * Representative media per pillar — the leading curated work of that pillar,
   * or `null`. See the "PILLAR MEDIA" note in the header for where it comes
   * from and why nothing new was authored to get it. `null` for both pillars is
   * the state the dataset is in today and renders the authored plate.
   */
  media?: Readonly<Partial<Record<Pillar, WorkEntrySummary | null>>>;
}

const { locale, services, media = {} } = Astro.props;
const copy = servicesIndexMessages(locale);

/**
 * The fork. `PILLARS` is the frozen order (`CONTENT_MODEL.md` §3: Architecture &
 * Design, then Reality Capture) and IA §3.2 forbids structural primacy of either
 * pillar — so the two groups are rendered identically, in the model's own order,
 * with no featured slot and no difference in weight. "Co-equal" is the word
 * NAV §5(1) uses.
 *
 * Both the slug and the name are required to publish in a locale
 * (`validation.ts`), and `serviceSummaries` has already dropped anything
 * unavailable here (§7.1, §11.2) — so the guards below are type guards, not
 * expected states. A Service missing either is dropped rather than rendered as a
 * dead row.
 */
const groups = PILLARS.map((pillar: Pillar) => ({
  pillar,
  label: pillarLabel(pillar, locale),
  hubHref: routePath(pillarRouteKey(pillar) as never, locale, undefined as never),
  cover: media[pillar]?.cover ?? null,
  /**
   * Whether the plate will end up **empty**, which is the only state the
   * graticule belongs in.
   *
   * `Plate` resolves its own media in three steps — Sanity cover, then the
   * visual-QA overlay, then the authored tone — and this component has to reach
   * the same answer to decide whether to draw an overlay on top of it. So it
   * asks the same function `Plate` asks, exactly as `ServicePage.astro` and
   * `Orientation.astro` both call `devServiceHero` rather than each guessing:
   * two components agreeing about one box is not optional, and the way to
   * guarantee it is one shared call, not two expressions that can drift.
   *
   * `devVisualImage` returns `null` in every build that did not opt in, so
   * without the flag this collapses to `cover === null` exactly as before.
   */
  empty:
    (media[pillar]?.cover ?? null) === null &&
    devVisualImage(devServicePool(pillar), `services-index:${pillar}`) === null,
  entries: services
    .filter((service) => service.pillar === pillar)
    .map((service) => {
      const slug = localize(service.slug, locale);
      const name = localize(service.name, locale);
      return slug && name ? { service, href: routePath('service', locale, slug) } : null;
    })
    .filter((entry): entry is NonNullable<typeof entry> => entry !== null),
})).filter((group) => group.entries.length > 0);

/**
 * ── NO COORDINATE RAIL (corrected at the visual-QA pass) ──────────────────
 * The first implementation rendered one, with a stop per group. It was removed,
 * for two reasons that are both contract, not taste.
 *
 * **The rail's page set excludes index pages.** `CoordinateRail.astro`:
 * "Present in HP, HUB, RC, WE and SV (**absent from WA**)", and `layout.css`
 * repeats it. The one page type the rail is authored *out* of is the Work
 * Archive — the browse/index surface. The Services index is that surface's
 * sibling: IA §2.5 gives it "orient & route", the same router responsibility,
 * and `WorkArchive.astro` accordingly carries no rail and no `SectionMarker`
 * stations either. Contact, the other HiFi-less page, has no rail for the same
 * reason. A rail here was inherited from borrowing the hub's rhythm, not from
 * the rail's own contract.
 *
 * **It measured something that is not authored.** Every other rail indexes a
 * fixed composition — the Work Entry's nine modules, the hub's six. This one's
 * length changed with the dataset: four stops with both pillars published, three
 * with one, two with none. A scale whose divisions depend on how much content an
 * editor happened to publish is not a measurement, and it implied an authored
 * structure this page does not have. MOTION_NOTES.md gives the rail a job —
 * tracking the active station — and there is no authored station sequence here
 * for it to track.
 *
 * The `data-station` attributes went with it: they exist only for the rail
 * tracker, and annotating nothing is worse than annotating nothing visibly. The
 * header's settle-to-solid is in the same runtime block but is guarded
 * separately, so it is unaffected.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  rhythm="pillar-hub"
  routeKey="services"
  title={copy.meta.title}
  description={copy.meta.description || undefined}
>
  <ViewTransition />

  {/* The masthead. Compact by construction: a label, the page's question, and
      the framing line if C has authored one. Nothing is reserved for content
      that does not exist. */}
  <section class="si-masthead" aria-labelledby="si-title">
    <p class="si-eyebrow eyebrow rv" data-rv="meta">{copy.eyebrow}</p>
    <h1 id="si-title" class="si-title rv" data-rv="head">{copy.heading}</h1>
    {copy.intro && <p class="si-intro rv" data-rv="meta">{copy.intro}</p>}
    {
      groups.length === 0 && copy.empty && (
        <p class="si-empty rv" data-rv="meta">
          {copy.empty}
        </p>
      )
    }
  </section>

  {/* The fork itself — one grid, two cells, so the two pillars are read
      together rather than one after the other. NAV §5(1) asks for "a co-equal
      pillar fork"; side by side is the composition that states it, and the
      Homepage's own M-2 fork is likewise a single grid holding both. */}
  {
    groups.length > 0 && (
      <div class="si-fork">
        {groups.map((group, index) => (
          <section
            class="si-group"
            data-pillar={group.pillar}
            aria-labelledby={`si-${group.pillar}`}
          >
            {/* ── THE PILLAR IS A HEADING, NOT A LABEL ────────────────────
                Composed here rather than with `SectionMarker`. The Marker's job
                is "labels one section" and its `.lbl` is the 11px mono label
                voice — correct where a section is *annotated*, wrong where the
                name IS the page's second-level hierarchy. On a two-cell fork the
                pillar name is the thing the visitor chooses between, so it takes
                the pixel display voice the site already gives every page-level
                name (`.wa-intro h1`, `.hub-arrival-lead h1`, `.si-title`), one
                step down from the page's own `<h1>`. No new voice, no new scale
                — the same `var(--pixel)` display role at a smaller clamp.

                The numeral stays blue and technical, above the name: the
                homepage's M-2 numbers its two co-equal capabilities the same way
                (`.cap .cnum`), and IA §3.2 allows an editorial order while
                forbidding structural primacy. Both cells render on one identical
                rule, so the order is a sequence and never an emphasis. The name
                wraps naturally at the column measure — it is never split by hand,
                because the label has exactly one declaration in `vocabulary.ts`. */}
            <p class="si-num" aria-hidden="true">
              {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
            </p>
            <h2 class="si-pillar rv" data-rv="head" id={`si-${group.pillar}`}>
              {group.label}
            </h2>

            {/* PILLAR MEDIA — a substantial region, at a fixed ratio, whatever
                fills it. `Plate` paints the curated cover when the CMS holds one;
                failing that, and only under `DEV_VISUAL_MEDIA`, the visual-QA
                overlay paints a temporary local image drawn from this pillar's
                own pool; failing both, the authored plate stays. **The geometry
                is identical in all three states** — the ratio is on this frame,
                not on the image — so the composition never collapses when a
                photograph is absent and never reflows when one arrives.

                The graticule is drawn only over the empty plate: it marks the
                box as instrument ground rather than as a missing asset, and
                laying it over a photograph would be an overlay neither HiFi
                authors outside Reality Capture.

                Decorative and inert throughout — the pillar is already named by
                the heading above it, so the plate repeats nothing into the
                accessible tree. */}
            <div class="si-plate rv" data-rv="img">
              {/* `priority`, i.e. `loading="eager"` + `fetchpriority="high"`.
                  Both pillar plates sit in the first viewport at every desktop
                  size — they are this page's LCP candidates and the whole point
                  of the composition. `Plate`'s default is `loading="lazy"`,
                  which is right for a card in a long archive and wrong here: it
                  left the second plate blank for the first seconds of a page
                  whose acceptance criterion is that both photographs are visible
                  without scrolling. Two images, both primary content — the same
                  call the Homepage's hero and the Work Entry's hero already make. */}
              <Plate
                image={group.cover}
                locale={locale}
                tone="var(--paper)"
                priority
                devKey={`services-index:${group.pillar}`}
                devPool={devServicePool(group.pillar)}
              />
              {group.empty && <span class="si-gridlay" aria-hidden="true" />}
            </div>

            <div class="si-rows">
              {group.entries.map((entry, position) => (
                <div class="rv" data-rv="meta">
                  <ServicePreviewCard
                    service={entry.service}
                    locale={locale}
                    href={entry.href}
                    index={position + 1}
                    cta={copy.cta}
                    variant="compact"
                  />
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            {/* Secondary, deliberately: NAV §5(2) keeps hubs "reachable and
                cross-linked" outside the global nav, while IA Step 6 (M1) keeps
                the hub off the Services task flow's critical path. It closes the
                cell, in the navigation voice, below the direct Service access it
                must not compete with. */}
            <p class="si-hub rv" data-rv="meta">
              <a href={group.hubHref}>
                {copy.hub.replace('{pillar}', group.label)}
                <span class="ar" aria-hidden="true">
                  →
                </span>
              </a>
            </p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
